/P 335 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
410 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 336 0 R
/T (Research Process & Insights)
/E (Research Process & Insights)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
411 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 336 0 R
/E (              During our iteration processes for Perspectives.ai, primary and secondary research was further expanded on, to successfully implement mentor feedback and user testing insights within our High-Fidelity Prototype. To begin, mentor feedback encouraged justification surrounding our service platform being a web-browser extension and its impact on our target users of young adults, in which previous iterations focused on distributing Perspectives.ai to detect potential biases on social media platforms. Moreover, our previous research explored news media bias within social media platforms however the feasibility of a web-browser based service solution lacked validity due a large portion of social media consumption being on smartphones compared to laptop devices. Thus, frequent feedback regarding the disconnect of our target's needs of detecting bias within social media and providing a web-based solution, guided our group to pivot by focusing on a different target group of university students utilizing laptop devices for their academic needs. In which, secondary research revealed the impact of web-browser solutions on university students utilizing their laptop devices for various academic needs \(Day, 2021\). Furthermore, research has revealed a positive impact of obtaining laptop devices for university students as it provides a platform to explore various academic sources when conducting online research. \(Deveci, 2018\). However, when looking at our target consumers we wanted to ensure we were providing a tool that was effective for university students within educational settings, in which research revealed limitations surrounding university students ability to recognize misinformation when consuming information online \(Nygren, 2020\). This allowed us to address mentor feedback regarding the value of Perspectives.ai, as our features provide users with the ability to detect micro and macro levels of information through our highlighting feature, in which Perspectives.ai allows users to gain a critical understanding through alternate sources provided from our service platform. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
412 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 336 0 R
/E (            Additionally, primary research was conducted to successfully address the feasibility of our service platform for university students academic needs. Thus, through usability testing \(Appendix pg. 32-34\), and qualitative data methods \(Appendix pg. 23\), feedback revealed how users valued a straightforward onboarding process, comfortable navigation, and easy access to the prototype features. Following this the High-Fidelity Prototype was iterated on to be utilized with or without the need to log in to a personal account, users now being able to access Perspectives.ai through the browser extension tab which was not applicable before, and users obtaining the Perspectives.ai action icon for quick access to the highlighting and general Perspectives.ai features. Moreover, user testing feedback addressed the feasibility of Perspectives.ai, with users appreciating the ability to recognize potential biases on a micro level through utilizing the highlighting feature of Perspectives.ai. Thus, the insights gained from our mentors and user-testing allowed us to refine our service platform to support our users and allow them to confidently engage with certain media information, whilst achieving balanced media information during their everyday browsing experiences. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
413 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 336 0 R
/E (5)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
414 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 337 0 R
/T (Feasibility)
/E (Feasibility)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
415 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 337 0 R
/E (     Throughout the stages of development our team has placed a major focus on ensuring the product is both feasible and realistic. Taking advantage of the recently emerging technologies of large language models, our solution leverages these technologies as an artificial second opinion, a system to evaluate the levels of bias and misinformation within a given set of text \(either highlighted or the entire article\). This makes for a relatively basic system beyond the LLM itself, as the extension need only function as a portal between the user and the server hosting the LLM, by gathering and sending information to the back end as well as receiving and displaying the server's response.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
416 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 337 0 R
/E (     As mentioned in previous submissions the team's eventual goal of developing our own language model is not yet realistic but something we expect may happen further down the line. With the development of tools such as IBMs Watson and other AI training models, it is becoming more and more feasible for Perspectives.ai to eventually go the route of producing its own AI, especially when considering the potential for outside help.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
417 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 337 0 R
/E (     Using feedback from both our professors as well as the mentors at IBM we chose to develop a solution that we could host locally, allowing for greater control and transparency of the technology. Alongside this hosting locally allows it to much easier control the costs associated with the API calls made by our extension rather than outsourcing that responsibility.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
